{"ratings":[{"id":"C1","consequence":"wrong","trigger":"plausible","reason":"Correcting or recreating a rejected runtime model leaves it unavailable until the block expires or the daemon restarts."},{"id":"C2","consequence":"wrong","trigger":"plausible","reason":"An operator cannot clear an assigned default effort through the JSON API, potentially preventing agent use until the model and its policy references are recreated."},{"id":"C3","consequence":"wrong","trigger":"plausible","reason":"An older probe can overwrite newer discovery after a provider is re-enabled, causing routing to reject a served model until another probe corrects the list."},{"id":"C4","consequence":"outage","trigger":"plausible","reason":"A persisted runtime model that collides with configuration or references a removed provider prevents the daemon from starting."},{"id":"C5","consequence":"nuisance","trigger":"plausible","reason":"The reproduced omission concerns explicitly disabled providers and leaves no skip line, without establishing an incorrect check selection for enabled providers."},{"id":"C6","consequence":"test","trigger":"normal","reason":"The test helper decodes the parameter itself, so the new tests do not exercise native HTTP route decoding, although the production route worked in the reproduction."},{"id":"C7","consequence":"test","trigger":"plausible","reason":"Slow child startup can make the cleanup fixture fail after a retry, but the same timing sensitivity exists in the base version."},{"id":"C8","consequence":"wrong","trigger":"plausible","reason":"Evaluating a model that discovery says is unavailable still invokes the harness and consumes a trial, potentially producing a misleading evaluation result."},{"id":"C5b","consequence":"outage","trigger":"plausible","reason":"Smoke applies checkout migrations to the live database before release acceptance, so subsequently editing an unapplied-release migration can cause later database opens and deploys to fail."}]}